commands.description:
Profile or check P(q) at powers of a primitive N-th root of unity. Without fixed counts or orbit sizes, only the exact root-of-unity evaluations are profiled.

Every run also reports the orbit decomposition of the polynomial (exact integer arithmetic; Alexandersson-Amini, 'The cone of cyclic sieving phenomena', Discrete Math. 342 (2019), arXiv:1804.01447):

  - reduce f modulo q^n-1 to residues c_0..c_{n-1}; f(zeta^d) is an integer for
    all d iff c_j = c_gcd(j,n) (Desarmenien); violating gcd classes are listed;
  - if integral, f = sum_(r|n) a_r h_r mod q^n-1 with h_r = 1+q^r+...+q^(n-r),
    a_r = sum_(e|r) mu(r/e) c_e = number of orbits of size n/r a CSP action needs;
    S_k = k*a_(n/k) = sum_(j|k) mu(k/j) f(zeta^j) elements lie in orbits of size k;
  - cone condition: all a_r >= 0 (equivalently all S_k >= 0). For f in N[q] this
    is necessary and sufficient for a C_n-action making (X, C_n, f) a CSP.

The hypothesis (natural coefficients) and the cone condition are reported separately. With --orbit-sizes the predicted orbit counts are compared with the actual ones. JSON adds `orbit_decomposition` and `orbit_sizes_check` next to the unchanged `report` of every item; exact integers are strings.
